    <h2>Common Pitfalls When Converting GMT to CEST</h2>

    <article class="mistake-card">
      <h3>1. Confusing UK Time (BST) with GMT Baseline</h3>
      <p>
        During summer, the UK uses BST (UTC+1). Comparing UK local time (BST) to CEST (UTC+2) yields a 1-hour gap, whereas comparing GMT baseline (UTC+0) to CEST yields a 2-hour gap.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="mistake-card">
      <h3>2. Confusing CEST (UTC+2) with Winter CET (UTC+1)</h3>
      <p>
        During winter, Central Europe moves to CET (UTC+1, 1 hour ahead of GMT). Applying CEST calculations (+2 hours) year-round causes a 1-hour error in winter.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="mistake-card">
      <h3>3. Overlooking Spanish Island Exceptions</h3>
      <p>
        While mainland Spain uses CEST (UTC+2), the Canary Islands observe WEST (UTC+1 in summer), creating a 1-hour difference within Spanish territory.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="mistake-card">
      <h3>4. Miscalculating Date Rollover for Late GMT Hours</h3>
      <p>
        GMT hours between 10:00 PM and 11:59 PM cross midnight in Paris, advancing to the next calendar date in CEST.
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>

